  ('33214e7f-4dcd-563e-bc8b-8363055a4650', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Which piece of evidence most directly supports the writer's claim that "shade makes a real difference in temperature"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"At noon, the grass under the maple read 29 degrees while the blacktop read 51.","is_correct":true},{"text":"On hot days, recess games end early according to the physical education teacher.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"At lunchtime, students crowd into the thin strip of shade by the building.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weaker-evidence"},{"text":"By lunchtime in spring, the slide on the playground is too hot to touch.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$At noon, the grass under the maple read 29 degrees while the blacktop read 51.$q$, $q$Only the class measurements compare a shaded spot and a sunny spot at the same moment, which directly shows the temperature difference. Students crowding into shade suggests it feels cooler, but it gives no measurement to prove it.$q$, 'draft', 'd2036755-cbe9-5988-b2da-662e71b2f27d')
